{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>וַתַּעֲנוּ אֹתִי, etc.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sifrei) that he said to them: ״You should have said: Our master, from whom is it fitting to learn, etc. — from you, for you labored over it, etc. — but I know,״ etc.; see there. And it is possible to interpret the verse as praise: Israel were pained by the matter, but for Moshe's benefit they suppressed their own feelings, and that is his saying <b>the thing is good</b> — to lighten the burden upon our master, even though it is hard for us to do, to learn from the mouth of your disciple; and that is his saying <b>which you spoke</b> — things that are hard for us to do them. And although the statement comes within the general framework of reproofs, perhaps he took offense even at this as well." "And it may be said that Moshe was wise to speak words that could be understood from them in two ways: one to their discredit, as they said in the Sifrei, and one as praise, as we have explained. And the reason is that within Israel there were two groups: one, the faction of the righteous, and one, the faction of the contentious; and each one knew himself. And when he spoke the words, each one would understand according to what was in his heart: the faction of the righteous — his words applied to them in our manner as I have explained, for they knew themselves what their intention had been; and the faction of the contentious — the words would pain them according to what they answered, and they would understand according to the interpretation of the Sifrei."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:14:15.995924841Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}